 `_status`. The underscore prefix signals "implementation detail, don\'t touch."\n- **Internal methods** like `_build_payload()`, `_run_tools()`, `_record_failure()`, `_execute_tool()` are clearly separated from public APIs.\n- **`ToolkitRegistry`** hides the three separate kit dictionaries (`_core_kits`, `_registered_kits`, `_available_kits`) behind clean properties: `schemas`, `skills`, `available_kits`.\n- **`Database`** wraps all raw SQL behind methods like `create_session()`, `get_messages()`, `add_message()`. No SQL strings leak into callers.\n- **`CompleteAgent._run_loop()`** encapsulates the entire agent loop internally; callers only see `run()` (which yields events) or `generate()` (which returns the final response).\n\n### Inheritance\n- **`BaseAgent` → `BasicAgent` / `CompleteAgent`**: The classic template-method pattern. `BaseAgent.generate()` calls `_build_user_message()` and `_build_payload()`, both of which children override.\n- **`ProgramError` hierarchy**: A clean exception tree that carries context (`name`, `kit_name`, `tool_name`) as you go deeper.\n- **`QAbstractListModel` → 4 list models**: Qt\'s model/view pattern. Each subclass overrides `rowCount()`, `data()`, and `roleNames()` to expose different data to QML.\n- **`QObject` → `AppController` / `CodeHighlighter` / `QmlReloader`**: All three inherit from Qt\'s base object to gain signal/slot machinery.\n\n### Polymorphism\n- **Duck-typed protocols** in `backend.py`:\n  ```python\n  class SessionsBackend(Protocol): ...\n  class AgentsBackend(Protocol): ...\n  ```\n  `MockAgentsBackend` and `MockSessionBackend` implement these protocols without explicitly inheriting from them. The controller works against the protocol, so you could swap in real HTTP backends without changing the controller.\n- **`BaseAgent._build_payload()`** is abstract and implemented differently:\n  - `BasicAgent`: fresh `[system, user]` — no history, no tools.\n  - `CompleteAgent`: injects tools schema and skills text, uses accumulated `_messages` history.\n- **`BaseAgent._build_user_message()`** has a default (tagged `[User name]:`) which `CompleteAgent` uses, but `BasicAgent` overrides to strip the tag entirely.\n- **`AgentEvent` union type**: the controller handles all event variants via pattern matching (`match event:`), treating each subtype polymorphically.\n- **`ToolResult[T]`** is generic: `ToolResult[None]`, `ToolResult[str]`, `ToolResult[dict[str, str]]` all used in different contexts.\n- **Sync/async tool dispatch** in `ToolkitRegistry.execute_tool()`: inspects the function and runs it directly or in a thread executor transparently.\n\n---\n\n## Known Issues & Limitations\n\n1. **All backends are labeled "Mock"** — `MockAgentsBackend` and `MockSessionBackend` are in production use. The protocols are clean, but only mock implementations exist. Real HTTP/API backends are stubs waiting to be written.\n\n2. **Streaming is half-built.** `Server._request_llm_streaming()` exists but is never called. `send_request_llm` only does non-streaming. Users see "Thinking…" with no incremental output until the full response arrives.\n\n3. **Tool failure → synthetic history entries.** `CompleteAgent._record_failure()` injects fake tool-call messages into history so the model can "see" what failed. This is clever for resilience but means failure artifacts pollute the message history, and the `RUN_ERROR_TOOL` dummy name is a hack.\n\n4. **System prompt re-injected every loop.** `CompleteAgent._build_payload()` builds `[system_message, *self._messages[1:]]` — discarding the original system message in `_messages[0]` and re-creating it with freshly injected skills text. Works, but means `_messages[0]` is dead weight after construction.\n\n5. **No built-in retry for LLM failures.** If OpenRouter returns an error, `LLMRequestError` propagates up and the agent run dies. The controller catches it and shows an error toast, but the user\'s input is lost.\n\n6. **Hardcoded DB path.** `data/agent.db` is resolved relative to the source file location via `../../../../data/agent.db`. Fragile if the project structure changes or the app is packaged.\n\n7. **No schema migrations.** The `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` approach means adding columns requires manual migration logic. Schema drift is inevitable over time.\n\n8. **`max_loop` vs `max_loops` naming inconsistency.** Pydantic field is `max_loop` (singular), but `CompleteAgent.__init__` calls it `max_loops` (plural). Works because they\'re separate layers, but it\'s confusing.\n\n9. **Race condition in `AppController._send()`.** Tasks are keyed by `session_id`. If the user switches sessions mid-generation, the old task keeps running in the background until the API call finishes — it\'s just de-registered from `_tasks`. No cancellation propagates to the HTTP layer.\n\n10. **QML hot-reloader is always on.** `QmlReloader` watches the filesystem with no debug flag. Harmless in production (just unused watchers) but unnecessary overhead.\n\n11. **`config/models.py` is empty.** The config module exists but has no models; `LLMParams` lives in `server/models.py` instead.\n\n12. **Minor naming quirks.** `database/erros.py` (typo), `ToolKitError` has inconsistent capitalization (mixed K), `_preview()` truncates tool arguments by character count which could cut mid-JSON.\n\n13. **Agent isolation is per-message-list, not truly secure.** `CompleteAgent._messages` is a flat list. History can contain unanswered tool calls from a previous failed run — the agent picks them up as warnings rather than being prevented from running.\n\n14. **No agent creation API at runtime.** Agent definitions are file-based: create a `.md` file in `definitions/` and restart. No way to register an agent programmatically.', created_at='Oct 05, 02:50 AM', speaker_name='Amadeus', attachments=()), MessageRow(id=290, session_id=19, role='user', text='yeah cuz now Im trying to make the README.md for this. so I need to know what are the important ones', created_at