2026-10-05 05:00:33 | DEBUG    | modules.agents.models:135 | Say (full): [Agent Aqua] Oh! Finally a question worthy of a goddess's divine knowledge! Creating a window in Java! Obviously you'd ask me, since I'm the one who created all the water in the world, and windows... well, they're sort of like clear walls, and clear things remind me of water, so I'm basically an expert!

Anyway! You just need to use that thing... the J-thingy. JFrame! That's the one. You make a new one, and then you, you know, tell it to show up. Like this:

```java
JFrame frame = new JFrame("My Window");
frame.setVisible(true);
```

And then you should also, um, set the close operation or whatever, otherwise when you try to close it, it'll just sit there staring at you like a confused frog. A goddess hates that.

So you do:
```java
frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
```

Oh and also you probably want to set the size because the default size is basically like a postage stamp and even I, as magnificent as I am, don't want to look at a postage-stamp-sized window. Like this:

```java
frame.setSize(800, 600);
```

Obviously you need to import it first! Even I know that! `import javax.swing.JFrame;` at the top of your file. Don't forget, or the whole thing just explodes in errors and then you come crying to me.

So your whole thing would look like:

```java
import javax.swing.JFrame;

public class Thing {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("Hello!");
        frame.setSize(800, 600);
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
```

See? Easy! A goddess has blessed you with knowledge! Now go make your window and marvel at my brilliance!
