2026-10-07 23:14:14 | DEBUG    | qasync.QThreadExecutor:204 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<function write_file at 0x7fce633dca90>, path='docs/arch_overview.typ', content='// ============================================================\n// UI & Reactive Architecture — Hotel Booking System\n// Generated from src/modules/gui/\n// ============================================================\n\n#import "@preview/fletcher:0.5.1" as fletcher\n#import "@preview/cetz:0.2.4" as cetz\n\n#set page(margin: 1.2in)\n#set text(font: ("Inter", "Noto Sans"), size: 11pt)\n#set heading(numbering: "1.1")\n\n#align(center)[#text(size: 22pt, weight: "bold", "GUI Architecture & Reactive Flow")]\n#align(center)[#text(size: 13pt, "Presentation-ready breakdown — UI to Signal Boundary")]\n\n#line(width: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)\n\n// ============================================================\n// SECTION 1 — OVERVIEW\n// ============================================================\n#heading[UI & Reactive Architecture Overview]\n\nThis document describes the frontend presentation layer of the hotel\nbooking application, from the top-level window down to the individual\ncomponents that fire signals into the backend. Everything here lives\nin #raw("src/modules/gui/") and speaks Qt Quick Markup Language (QML).\n\n#heading(level: 2)[The Big Picture]\n\nThe application is structured as a *single-window, page-switching*\narchitecture. A root #raw("Main.qml") opens a #raw("Window") containing a\n#raw("Loader") that loads #raw("App.qml"). Inside #raw("App.qml"), every\n"screen" is a separate QML component stacked inside a #raw("StackLayout").\nOnly one page is visible at a time — the #raw("currentPage") property\ndetermines which one.\n\nWhen the user interacts with a component (clicks a button, fills a form,\ntoggles a chip), the component emits a **QML Signal**. That signal either:\n\n- Flies to #raw("App.qml") to switch pages (pure UI navigation), or\n- Calls a #raw("pyqtSlot") on the #raw("Controller") object, which crosses\n  the boundary into Python backend code.\n\n*This document stops at that boundary.* We describe the components and\nthe signals they fire — not what happens after the signal lands in Python.\n\n#heading(level: 2)[The Beginner\'s Analogy]\n\n> Think of the UI like the dashboard of a hotel front desk. The\n> *views and components* are the screens, buttons, and forms you\n> see and touch. The *signals* are the buttons themselves — when\n> you press a call bell, a signal fires inside the desk. Someone\n> behind the counter hears it and acts. We are documenting the\n> screens, the buttons, and what each button says when pressed.\n> What happens *after* the bell rings is a different room.\n\n#align(center, [#text(size: 10pt, style: "italic", "Diagram 1 — Views contain Components, Components fire Signals")])\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  // --- Layer 1: Views ---\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", "Views (Pages)")\n    #v(0.1em)\n    #text(size: 8pt, "HomePage.qml, RoomListPage.qml,")\n    #text(size: 8pt, "ReservationForm.qml, LoginPage.qml ...")\n  ]),\n  arrow(dy: -1),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", "Components")\n    #v(0.1em)\n    #text(size: 8pt, "RoomCard, Header, FormField,")\n    #text(size: 8pt, "AccentButton, PillButton, FilterChip ...")\n  ]),\n  arrow(dy: -1),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, fill: luma(220), text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", "▸ SIGNAL BOUNDARY ◂")\n    #v(0.1em)\n    #text(size: 8pt, "clicked, bookRequested, navigateRequested,")\n    #text(size: 8pt, "login(), register(), bookRoom() ...")\n  ]),\n  #text(size: 9pt, fill: blue, "[ Signal stops here — backend is beyond this line ]")\n)\n\n#pagebreak\n\n// ============================================================\n// SECTION 2 — GUI & VIEW BREAKDOWN\n// ============================================================\n#heading[GUI & View Breakdown]\n\nEvery subsection below describes one major screen in the application.\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Main.qml — Application Entry Point]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/Main.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Window + Loader → bootstraps App.qml],\n)\n\n*Purpose (The \'What\'):* Creates the top-level window, sets its title,\ndimensions, and icon. A #raw("Loader") component reads #raw("App.qml") from\ndisk and instantiates it. Also wires a Python-side #raw("reloader") object\nthat, when triggered, clears the QML engine cache and re-loads\n#raw("App.qml") — useful for hot-reloading during development.\n\n*Beginner Example:* Think of #raw("Main.qml") as the front door and\nentry hall of a building. It opens the door (creates the window) and\npoints you to the main lobby (#raw("App.qml")). The reloader is like a\nlight switch that resets the lobby layout without leaving the building.\n\n*Child Components:* #raw("Window"), #raw("Loader").\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[App.qml — Page Router / Shell]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/App.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Master shell — owns page switching],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* The central orchestrator. It imports every page component,\nstacks them in a #raw("StackLayout"), and exposes a #raw("currentPage")\nstring property (default: "Home"). A #raw("Connections") block listens for\n#raw("navigateRequested") signals from child pages and sets\n#raw("currentPage"). Each page\'s #raw("visible") property is bound to\n#raw("currentPage") — only one is shown at a time.\n\nAlso holds a reference to the #raw("controller") object (passed in from\nPython), which it forwards to every child page via property bindings.\n\n*Beginner Example:* #raw("App.qml") is like a TV remote\'s input selector.\nYou press "HDMI 1" and only that input shows on screen. The remote\ndoesn\'t know what\'s playing — it just switches the visible channel.\n\n*Child Components:*\n- Header (#raw("generic/Header.qml"))\n- HomePage\n- RoomListPage\n- ReservationForm\n- ReservationListPage\n- LoginPage\n- RegisterPage\n- AboutPage\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Header.qml — Top Navigation Bar]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/generic/Header.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Persistent nav bar on every page],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* Displays the hotel logo, a row of navigation links (Home,\nRooms, Reserve, Reservations, About), and login/register buttons — or if\nlogged in, a user-greeting and Logout button. Visibility of auth buttons\nvs. user info is driven by #raw("controller.isLoggedIn").\n\n*Beginner Example:* The header is like a restaurant menu board at the\ntop of every table — you can always see what\'s available, and the\n"specials" change based on whether you\'re a known customer.\n\n*Child Components:* #raw("NavLink") (×5), #raw("AccentButton") (Login,\nRegister), user label, Logout button.\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[HomePage.qml — Landing / Room Preview]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/home_page/HomePage.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Welcome screen + room catalogue preview],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* Shows a hero-like welcome section with a background image,\nthe hotel name, and a tagline. Below that, a grid of #raw("RoomCard")\ncomponents (one per room) pulled from #raw("controller.roomListModel").\nEach card shows an image, room number, tier badge, price, and a hover\noverlay with star rating + recent reviews + a "Book now" button.\n\n*Beginner Example:* This is like the lobby of a hotel — a beautiful\nentrance with brochures of every room type laid out on a table. You\nbrowse, see prices, and pick one to learn more or book.\n\n*Child Components:* #raw("RoomCard") (×N, one per room).\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[RoomListPage.qml — Filtered Room Search]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/room_list_page/RoomListPage.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Filter + browse all rooms],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* Shows a filter panel on the left (tier filter chips, date\nrange, guest count, price range) and a grid of #raw("RoomCard") on the\nright. The Apply button calls #raw("controller.setRoomFilters(...)") and\nrefreshes the room list. Clear resets filters. Inline\ncomponents include #raw("FilterChip") (toggleable pill) and\n#raw("FilterField") (labelled text input).\n\n*Beginner Example:* This is like a hotel booking website\'s search\nresults page — checkboxes and sliders on the left, room cards on the\nright. You tweak what you want and hit "Apply" to see matching rooms.\n\n*Child Components:* #raw("RoomCard") (×N), #raw("FilterChip") (×M),\n#raw("FilterField") (×4), #raw("AccentButton") (Apply, Clear).\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[ReservationForm.qml — Booking Form]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/reservation_form/ReservationForm.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") New reservation / booking form],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* A multi-section form: (1) selected room card at the top,\n(2) guest details (name, email, phone) with some fields auto-filled from\n#raw("controller.currentUser*"), (3) booking dates (check-in, check-out),\n(4) a summary section showing price breakdown, and (5) payment method\nselection (Online / At Counter). A "Confirm Booking" button at the\nbottom calls #raw("controller.bookRoom(...)") and then\n#raw("controller.pay(...)").\n\nInline components: #raw("FormField") (×5), #raw("SummaryRow") (×4),\n#raw("PaymentOption") (×2), #raw("PillButton"), #raw("RoomCard").\n\n*Beginner Example:* This is the paper form you fill out at a hotel\nfront desk — name, dates, room type, how you\'ll pay. The computer\npre-fills what it knows and you fill in the rest, then hand it to the\nclerk (the "Confirm" button).\n\n*Child Components:* #raw("RoomCard"), #raw("FormField") (×5),\n#raw("SummaryRow") (×4), #raw("PaymentOption") (×2), #raw("PillButton").\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[ReservationListPage.qml — My Reservations]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/reservation_page/ReservationListPage.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") View / cancel existing bookings],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* If logged in, shows a list of the user\'s reservations\n(room, dates, status, amount paid, balance due). Each row has a Cancel\nbutton that calls #raw("controller.cancelReservation(...)"). If not\nlogged in, shows a prompt with a link to the Login page.\n\nListens for #raw("controller.currentUserChanged") to re-fetch\nreservations when the user logs in/out.\n\n*Beginner Example:* This is like the "My Trips" page on a travel\napp — a list of everything you\'ve booked, with the option to cancel\nif your plans change.\n\n*Child Components:* ListView rows (inline), #raw("AccentButton") (Cancel),\n#raw("NavLink") (to Login).\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[LoginPage.qml & RegisterPage.qml — Auth Screens]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Files:") #raw("src/modules/gui/auth_page/LoginPage.qml") & #raw("RegisterPage.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Authentication forms],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* #raw("LoginPage") has email + password fields and a Login\nbutton that calls #raw("controller.login(...)"). #raw("RegisterPage") has\nfull name, email, phone, password, and confirm-password fields, with a\nRegister button that calls #raw("controller.register(...)"). Both show\nerror messages in a label if the backend returns an error string.\n\n*Beginner Example:* This is the check-in kiosk at the airport — type\nyour credentials (or create an account) to prove who you are. The\nscreen tells you if something\'s wrong (wrong password, email taken).\n\n*Child Components (both):* #raw("FormField") (×2–4), #raw("PillButton"),\nerror label, nav link to the other auth page.\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[AboutPage.qml — Info Screen]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/about_page/AboutPage.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Static about / contact page],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* Displays static information about the hotel (description,\ncontact details, address). No interactive signals — purely informational.\n\n*Beginner Example:* The "About Us" page on any website — you read it,\nyou don\'t interact with it.\n\n*Child Components:* None (static text and images only).\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Reusable Components (Generic)]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 3,\n  rows: 3,\n  gutter: 0.2in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold")[Component], #text(weight: "bold")[File], #text(weight: "bold")[Role]],\n  #raw("RoomCard"), #raw("generic/RoomCard.qml"), "Room preview card with hover overlay + Book button",\n  #raw("NavLink"), #raw("generic/NavLink.qml"), "Nav link with animated underline",\n  #raw("AccentButton"), #raw("generic/AccentButton.qml"), "Gold rounded call-to-action button",\n  #raw("CustomButton"), #raw("generic/CustomButton.qml"), "Base button (text + font size)",\n  #raw("CustomRect"), #raw("generic/CustomRect.qml"), "Debug rectangle (magenta border)",\n  #raw("SearchField"), #raw("generic/SearchField.qml"), "Label + value chip (emits clicked)",\n)\n\n#pagebreak\n\n// ============================================================\n// SECTION 3 — COMPONENT TO SIGNAL FLOW\n// ============================================================\n#heading[Component to Signal Flow Mapping]\n\nThis section maps every interactive component to the signal or slot it\ntriggers. The table below is the complete inventory of "what fires what."\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Signal Map]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 4,\n  rows: 15,\n  gutter: 0.15in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold")[Component], #text(weight: "bold")[Signal / Slot], #text(weight: "bold")[Direction], #text(weight: "bold")[Beginner Trigger Explanation]],\n  //\n  #raw("NavLink"), #raw("navigateRequested(page)"), "→ App.qml", "You click a nav link (Home, Rooms, etc.) → the app switches to that page.",\n  //\n  #raw("AccentButton"), #raw("clicked"), "→ parent page", "You click a gold button → the page that owns it hears the click and decides what to do.",\n  //\n  #raw("RoomCard"), #raw("bookRequested(roomNum, ...)"), "→ RoomListPage", "You click \'Book now\' on a room card → the room number and dates are sent up to open the reservation form.",\n  //\n  #raw("RoomCard"), #raw("clicked"), "→ parent page", "You click the card body (not the button) → the parent page could react (e.g. show details).",\n  //\n  #raw("PillButton"), #raw("clicked"), "→ parent page", "You press a pill-shaped button (Login, Register, Confirm) → the parent form reads inputs and calls a controller slot.",\n  //\n  #raw("FilterChip"), #raw("clicked"), "→ RoomListPage", "You toggle a tier chip → the page updates its internal filter state.",\n  //\n  #raw("SearchField"), #raw("clicked"), "→ parent page", "You click a search chip → the parent page could open a date picker or dropdown.",\n  //\n  #raw("FormField"), "(none — passive input)", "—", "You type into a text field. The field itself emits no signal. The parent button reads its #raw(\\"text\\") property when clicked.",\n  //\n  #raw("SummaryRow"), "(none — passive label)", "—", "Displays a calculated value. No signal.",\n  //\n  #raw("PaymentOption"), #raw("clicked"), "→ ReservationForm", "You select a payment method (Online / At Counter) → the form remembers your choice for the booking call.",\n  //\n  #raw("Login form"), #raw("controller.login(email, pass)"), "→ Python slot", "You fill in email + password and press Login → the controller checks credentials and returns an error string (empty = success).",\n  //\n  #raw("Register form"), #raw("controller.register(...)"), "→ Python slot", "You fill in name, email, phone, password and press Register → the controller creates the account.",\n  //\n  #raw("Logout button"), #raw("controller.logout()"), "→ Python slot", "You click Logout → the controller clears the current user session.",\n  //\n  #raw("Cancel button"), #raw("controller.cancelReservation(num)"), "→ Python slot", "You click Cancel on a reservation → the controller cancels it in the database.",\n)\n\n#heading(level: 2)[State Properties (Read by QML, Written by Python)]\n\nThese aren\'t signals, but they drive what the UI *shows*:\n\n| Property | Written By | Read By |\n|---|---|---|\n| #raw("controller.isLoggedIn") | Python (login/logout) | Header, ReservationListPage |\n| #raw("controller.currentUserName") | Python (login/register) | Header, ReservationForm |\n| #raw("controller.currentUserEmail") | Python (login/register) | ReservationForm |\n| #raw("controller.roomListModel") | Python (refreshRooms) | HomePage, RoomListPage, ReservationForm |\n| #raw("controller.roomTierListModel") | Python (refreshTiers) | RoomListPage |\n| #raw("controller.reservationListModel") | Python (refreshReservations) | ReservationListPage |\n\nThe only QML-emitted *signal* that Python sends back is:\n\n- #raw("controller.currentUserChanged") — emitted after login/logout/profile-change, so the QML reservation page knows to reload.\n\n#pagebreak\n\n// ============================================================\n// SECTION 4 — UI FLOW & STATE BOUNDARY DIAGRAMS\n// ============================================================\n#heading[UI Flow & State Boundary Diagrams]\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 1: Application Startup]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("Main.qml", shape: fletcher.round, text: "Main.qml"),\n  arrow("Window + Loader"),\n  node("App.qml", shape: fletcher.round, text: "App.qml", fill: luma(230)),\n  arrow("currentPage = \\"Home\\""),\n  node("HomePage\\nRoomCard grid", fill: luma(245), text: "HomePage.qml"),\n)\n\n*What happens:* #raw("Main.qml") creates the window and loads\n#raw("App.qml"). #raw("App.qml") sets the initial page to "Home", making\n#raw("HomePage.qml") visible. The home page reads\n#raw("controller.roomListModel") to populate its grid of #raw("RoomCard")\ncomponents. *No user interaction yet — pure bootstrap.*\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 2: Navigation (Page Switching)]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("Header.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "Header.qml\\nNavLink(\\"Rooms\\")"),\n  arrow("navigateRequested(\\"Rooms\\")"),\n  node("App.qml", fill: luma(230), text: "App.qml\\ncurrentPage = \\"Rooms\\""),\n  arrow("→ RoomListPage visible"),\n  node("RoomListPage.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "RoomListPage.qml"),\n)\n\n*Boundary:* The signal stops at #raw("App.qml"). No Python code is\ninvolved — pure QML-to-QML navigation. The signal payload is a string\n(the page name), and the router sets visibility accordingly.\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 3: Login (Crosses Signal Boundary)]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("LoginPage.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "LoginPage.qml\\nFormField × 2\\nPillButton"),\n  arrow("controller.login(email, password)"),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, fill: luma(220), text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", fill: red, "▸ SIGNAL BOUNDARY ◂")\n    #text(size: 8pt, fill: blue, "QML → Python")\n  ]),\n  arrow("returns error string"),\n  node("LoginPage.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "LoginPage.qml\\n(error label shows if non-empty)"),\n)\n\n*What happens:*\n1. User types email + password into #raw("FormField") components.\n2. User clicks the #raw("PillButton") ("Login").\n3. The button\'s #raw("onClicked") handler reads the text from both\n   fields and calls #raw("controller.login(email, password)").\n4. This crosses the **Signal Boundary** — a #raw("pyqtSlot") in Python\n   runs authentication logic.\n5. Python returns a string: empty means success, non-empty is an error.\n6. If non-empty, the error label on the page becomes visible with the\n   message. If empty, the controller sets #raw("currentUser") and emits\n   #raw("currentUserChanged"), and the page navigates to Home.\n\n*Beginner Explanation:* You type your username and password (like\nswiping a keycard). Pressing "Login" is like pressing the keycard\nagainst the reader. The reader (the controller slot) checks if it\'s\nvalid and either opens the door (success → navigate home) or beeps\nred (error label).\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 4: Booking a Room (Multi-Step, Multiple Boundaries)]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("RoomCard", fill: luma(245), text: "RoomCard.qml\\n\'Book now\' button"),\n  arrow("bookRequested(roomNum)"),\n  node("RoomListPage", fill: luma(245), text: "RoomListPage\\n→ navigateRequested(\\"Reserve\\")"),\n  arrow("opens ReservationForm"),\n  node("ReservationForm.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "ReservationForm.qml\\nRoomCard + FormFields + PaymentOption"),\n  arrow("controller.bookRoom(...)"),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, fill: luma(220), text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", fill: red, "▸ SIGNAL BOUNDARY ◂")\n  ]),\n  arrow("success"),\n  node("ReservationForm", fill: luma(245), text: "ReservationForm\\n→ navigateRequested(\\"Reservations\\")"),\n)\n\n*What happens (step by step):*\n1. On HomePage or RoomListPage, user hovers a #raw("RoomCard") and\n   clicks "Book now".\n2. The card emits #raw("bookRequested(roomNum, ...)"). The parent page\n   catches it, calls #raw("App.qml") to navigate to "Reserve", and\n   opens #raw("ReservationForm.qml") with the selected room pre-filled.\n3. User fills in guest details, dates, and picks a payment method.\n4. User clicks "Confirm Booking" → the form calls:\n   - #raw("controller.bookRoom(userId, roomNum, dateIn, dateOut)")\n   - #raw("controller.pay(reservationNum, method, amount)")\n   Both cross the **Signal Boundary** into Python.\n5. On success, the form navigates to the Reservations page so the user\n   can see their new booking.\n\n*Beginner Explanation:* You see a room you like in a brochure (RoomCard).\nYou tear off the slip and hand it to the front desk (bookRequested).\nThe desk gives you a form to fill (ReservationForm). You fill in your\ndetails and hand it back (controller.bookRoom). The clerk processes it\nand hands you a receipt. All the "handing off" moments are signals.\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 5: Filtering Rooms]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("RoomListPage.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "RoomListPage.qml\\nFilterChip × N\\nFilterField × 4"),\n  arrow("clicked (tier chip)"),\n  node("RoomListPage", fill: luma(245), text: "(internal state updated)"),\n  arrow("Apply → controller.setRoomFilters(...)"),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, fill: luma(220), text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", fill: red, "▸ SIGNAL BOUNDARY ◂")\n  ]),\n  arrow("Python refreshes roomListModel"),\n  node("RoomListPage", fill: luma(245), text: "RoomCard grid auto-updates\\n(via model binding)"),\n)\n\n*What happens:*\n1. User toggles tier chips and types in date/price fields. Each chip\n   click updates an internal variable; no signal crosses yet.\n2. User clicks "Apply". The page reads all filter values and calls\n   #raw("controller.setRoomFilters(...)") — crossing the boundary.\n3. Python processes the filters, updates #raw("controller.roomListModel").\n4. The #raw("RoomCard") grid, bound to the model, automatically shows\n   the new filtered set. (Qt\'s model/view binding handles this — no\n   extra signal needed.)\n\n*Beginner Explanation:* You\'re using the sliders and checkboxes on a\ncar rental website. You tweak things — nothing happens yet. You click\n"Search" (Apply), and the results update. The tweaks are local, the\nsearch crosses into the database.\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 6: Reservation Cancellation]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("ReservationListPage.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "ReservationListPage.qml\\nCancel button"),\n  arrow("controller.cancelReservation(num)"),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, fill: luma(220), text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", fill: red, "▸ SIGNAL BOUNDARY ◂")\n  ]),\n  arrow("Python refreshes reservationListModel"),\n  node("ReservationListPage", fill: luma(245), text: "List auto-updates"),\n)\n\n*What happens:* User clicks Cancel → slot call → Python deletes from\nDB → model refreshes → QML list view reflects the change. Simple,\nsingle-boundary crossing.\n\n---\n\n// ============================================================\n// SUMMARY DIAGRAM — Full Page Map\n// ============================================================\n#pagebreak\n#heading[Appendix: Full Navigation Map]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("Main.qml", shape: fletcher.round, text: "Main.qml"),\n  arrow(),\n  node("App.qml", shape: fletcher.round, fill: luma(225), text: "App.qml\\n(currentPage router)"),\n  //\n  arrow(dx: -3.5, dy: -1.5, label: "Home"),\n  node("HomePage.qml\\n+ RoomCard ×N", fill: luma(245)),\n  //\n  arrow(dx: 0, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 3, label: "Rooms"),\n  node("RoomListPage.qml\\n+ FilterChips + RoomCard ×N", fill: luma(245)),\n  arrow(dx: 3.5, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 4, label: "Reserve"),\n  node("ReservationForm.qml\\n+ FormFields + PaymentOption", fill: luma(245)),\n  arrow(dx: 0, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 5, label: "Reservations"),\n  node("ReservationListPage.qml\\n+ ListView + Cancel buttons", fill: luma(245)),\n  arrow(dx: -3.5, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 6, label: "Login"),\n  node("LoginPage.qml\\n+ FormFields + PillButton", fill: luma(245)),\n  arrow(dx: 0, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 7, label: "Register"),\n  node("RegisterPage.qml\\n+ FormFields + PillButton", fill: luma(245)),\n  arrow(dx: 3.5, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 8, label: "About"),\n  node("AboutPage.qml\\n(static)", fill: luma(245)),\n)\n\n#align(center, [#text(size: 9pt, style: "italic", "Every arrow is a navigateRequested() signal. Only one page is visible at a time.")])\n\n---\n\n#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray, "Generated from src/modules/gui/ — architecture document for presentation.")') with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
2026-10-07 23:14:14 | DEBUG    | qasync._QThreadWorker:132 | #4 got callback functools.partial(<function write_file at 0x7fce633dca90>, path='docs/arch_overview.typ', content='// ============================================================\n// UI & Reactive Architecture — Hotel Booking System\n// Generated from src/modules/gui/\n// ============================================================\n\n#import "@preview/fletcher:0.5.1" as fletcher\n#import "@preview/cetz:0.2.4" as cetz\n\n#set page(margin: 1.2in)\n#set text(font: ("Inter", "Noto Sans"), size: 11pt)\n#set heading(numbering: "1.1")\n\n#align(center)[#text(size: 22pt, weight: "bold", "GUI Architecture & Reactive Flow")]\n#align(center)[#text(size: 13pt, "Presentation-ready breakdown — UI to Signal Boundary")]\n\n#line(width: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)\n\n// ============================================================\n// SECTION 1 — OVERVIEW\n// ============================================================\n#heading[UI & Reactive Architecture Overview]\n\nThis document describes the frontend presentation layer of the hotel\nbooking application, from the top-level window down to the individual\ncomponents that fire signals into the backend. Everything here lives\nin #raw("src/modules/gui/") and speaks Qt Quick Markup Language (QML).\n\n#heading(level: 2)[The Big Picture]\n\nThe application is structured as a *single-window, page-switching*\narchitecture. A root #raw("Main.qml") opens a #raw("Window") containing a\n#raw("Loader") that loads #raw("App.qml"). Inside #raw("App.qml"), every\n"screen" is a separate QML component stacked inside a #raw("StackLayout").\nOnly one page is visible at a time — the #raw("currentPage") property\ndetermines which one.\n\nWhen the user interacts with a component (clicks a button, fills a form,\ntoggles a chip), the component emits a **QML Signal**. That signal either:\n\n- Flies to #raw("App.qml") to switch pages (pure UI navigation), or\n- Calls a #raw("pyqtSlot") on the #raw("Controller") object, which crosses\n  the boundary into Python backend code.\n\n*This document stops at that boundary.* We describe the components and\nthe signals they fire — not what happens after the signal lands in Python.\n\n#heading(level: 2)[The Beginner\'s Analogy]\n\n> Think of the UI like the dashboard of a hotel front desk. The\n> *views and components* are the screens, buttons, and forms you\n> see and touch. The *signals* are the buttons themselves — when\n> you press a call bell, a signal fires inside the desk. Someone\n> behind the counter hears it and acts. We are documenting the\n> screens, the buttons, and what each button says when pressed.\n> What happens *after* the bell rings is a different room.\n\n#align(center, [#text(size: 10pt, style: "italic", "Diagram 1 — Views contain Components, Components fire Signals")])\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  // --- Layer 1: Views ---\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", "Views (Pages)")\n    #v(0.1em)\n    #text(size: 8pt, "HomePage.qml, RoomListPage.qml,")\n    #text(size: 8pt, "ReservationForm.qml, LoginPage.qml ...")\n  ]),\n  arrow(dy: -1),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", "Components")\n    #v(0.1em)\n    #text(size: 8pt, "RoomCard, Header, FormField,")\n    #text(size: 8pt, "AccentButton, PillButton, FilterChip ...")\n  ]),\n  arrow(dy: -1),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, fill: luma(220), text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", "▸ SIGNAL BOUNDARY ◂")\n    #v(0.1em)\n    #text(size: 8pt, "clicked, bookRequested, navigateRequested,")\n    #text(size: 8pt, "login(), register(), bookRoom() ...")\n  ]),\n  #text(size: 9pt, fill: blue, "[ Signal stops here — backend is beyond this line ]")\n)\n\n#pagebreak\n\n// ============================================================\n// SECTION 2 — GUI & VIEW BREAKDOWN\n// ============================================================\n#heading[GUI & View Breakdown]\n\nEvery subsection below describes one major screen in the application.\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Main.qml — Application Entry Point]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/Main.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Window + Loader → bootstraps App.qml],\n)\n\n*Purpose (The \'What\'):* Creates the top-level window, sets its title,\ndimensions, and icon. A #raw("Loader") component reads #raw("App.qml") from\ndisk and instantiates it. Also wires a Python-side #raw("reloader") object\nthat, when triggered, clears the QML engine cache and re-loads\n#raw("App.qml") — useful for hot-reloading during development.\n\n*Beginner Example:* Think of #raw("Main.qml") as the front door and\nentry hall of a building. It opens the door (creates the window) and\npoints you to the main lobby (#raw("App.qml")). The reloader is like a\nlight switch that resets the lobby layout without leaving the building.\n\n*Child Components:* #raw("Window"), #raw("Loader").\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[App.qml — Page Router / Shell]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/App.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Master shell — owns page switching],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* The central orchestrator. It imports every page component,\nstacks them in a #raw("StackLayout"), and exposes a #raw("currentPage")\nstring property (default: "Home"). A #raw("Connections") block listens for\n#raw("navigateRequested") signals from child pages and sets\n#raw("currentPage"). Each page\'s #raw("visible") property is bound to\n#raw("currentPage") — only one is shown at a time.\n\nAlso holds a reference to the #raw("controller") object (passed in from\nPython), which it forwards to every child page via property bindings.\n\n*Beginner Example:* #raw("App.qml") is like a TV remote\'s input selector.\nYou press "HDMI 1" and only that input shows on screen. The remote\ndoesn\'t know what\'s playing — it just switches the visible channel.\n\n*Child Components:*\n- Header (#raw("generic/Header.qml"))\n- HomePage\n- RoomListPage\n- ReservationForm\n- ReservationListPage\n- LoginPage\n- RegisterPage\n- AboutPage\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Header.qml — Top Navigation Bar]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/generic/Header.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Persistent nav bar on every page],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* Displays the hotel logo, a row of navigation links (Home,\nRooms, Reserve, Reservations, About), and login/register buttons — or if\nlogged in, a user-greeting and Logout button. Visibility of auth buttons\nvs. user info is driven by #raw("controller.isLoggedIn").\n\n*Beginner Example:* The header is like a restaurant menu board at the\ntop of every table — you can always see what\'s available, and the\n"specials" change based on whether you\'re a known customer.\n\n*Child Components:* #raw("NavLink") (×5), #raw("AccentButton") (Login,\nRegister), user label, Logout button.\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[HomePage.qml — Landing / Room Preview]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/home_page/HomePage.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Welcome screen + room catalogue preview],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* Shows a hero-like welcome section with a background image,\nthe hotel name, and a tagline. Below that, a grid of #raw("RoomCard")\ncomponents (one per room) pulled from #raw("controller.roomListModel").\nEach card shows an image, room number, tier badge, price, and a hover\noverlay with star rating + recent reviews + a "Book now" button.\n\n*Beginner Example:* This is like the lobby of a hotel — a beautiful\nentrance with brochures of every room type laid out on a table. You\nbrowse, see prices, and pick one to learn more or book.\n\n*Child Components:* #raw("RoomCard") (×N, one per room).\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[RoomListPage.qml — Filtered Room Search]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/room_list_page/RoomListPage.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Filter + browse all rooms],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* Shows a filter panel on the left (tier filter chips, date\nrange, guest count, price range) and a grid of #raw("RoomCard") on the\nright. The Apply button calls #raw("controller.setRoomFilters(...)") and\nrefreshes the room list. Clear resets filters. Inline\ncomponents include #raw("FilterChip") (toggleable pill) and\n#raw("FilterField") (labelled text input).\n\n*Beginner Example:* This is like a hotel booking website\'s search\nresults page — checkboxes and sliders on the left, room cards on the\nright. You tweak what you want and hit "Apply" to see matching rooms.\n\n*Child Components:* #raw("RoomCard") (×N), #raw("FilterChip") (×M),\n#raw("FilterField") (×4), #raw("AccentButton") (Apply, Clear).\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[ReservationForm.qml — Booking Form]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/reservation_form/ReservationForm.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") New reservation / booking form],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* A multi-section form: (1) selected room card at the top,\n(2) guest details (name, email, phone) with some fields auto-filled from\n#raw("controller.currentUser*"), (3) booking dates (check-in, check-out),\n(4) a summary section showing price breakdown, and (5) payment method\nselection (Online / At Counter). A "Confirm Booking" button at the\nbottom calls #raw("controller.bookRoom(...)") and then\n#raw("controller.pay(...)").\n\nInline components: #raw("FormField") (×5), #raw("SummaryRow") (×4),\n#raw("PaymentOption") (×2), #raw("PillButton"), #raw("RoomCard").\n\n*Beginner Example:* This is the paper form you fill out at a hotel\nfront desk — name, dates, room type, how you\'ll pay. The computer\npre-fills what it knows and you fill in the rest, then hand it to the\nclerk (the "Confirm" button).\n\n*Child Components:* #raw("RoomCard"), #raw("FormField") (×5),\n#raw("SummaryRow") (×4), #raw("PaymentOption") (×2), #raw("PillButton").\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[ReservationListPage.qml — My Reservations]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/reservation_page/ReservationListPage.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") View / cancel existing bookings],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* If logged in, shows a list of the user\'s reservations\n(room, dates, status, amount paid, balance due). Each row has a Cancel\nbutton that calls #raw("controller.cancelReservation(...)"). If not\nlogged in, shows a prompt with a link to the Login page.\n\nListens for #raw("controller.currentUserChanged") to re-fetch\nreservations when the user logs in/out.\n\n*Beginner Example:* This is like the "My Trips" page on a travel\napp — a list of everything you\'ve booked, with the option to cancel\nif your plans change.\n\n*Child Components:* ListView rows (inline), #raw("AccentButton") (Cancel),\n#raw("NavLink") (to Login).\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[LoginPage.qml & RegisterPage.qml — Auth Screens]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Files:") #raw("src/modules/gui/auth_page/LoginPage.qml") & #raw("RegisterPage.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Authentication forms],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* #raw("LoginPage") has email + password fields and a Login\nbutton that calls #raw("controller.login(...)"). #raw("RegisterPage") has\nfull name, email, phone, password, and confirm-password fields, with a\nRegister button that calls #raw("controller.register(...)"). Both show\nerror messages in a label if the backend returns an error string.\n\n*Beginner Example:* This is the check-in kiosk at the airport — type\nyour credentials (or create an account) to prove who you are. The\nscreen tells you if something\'s wrong (wrong password, email taken).\n\n*Child Components (both):* #raw("FormField") (×2–4), #raw("PillButton"),\nerror label, nav link to the other auth page.\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[AboutPage.qml — Info Screen]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 2,\n  rows: 1,\n  gutter: 0.3in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "File:") #raw("src/modules/gui/about_page/AboutPage.qml")],\n  [#text(weight: "bold", "Role:") Static about / contact page],\n)\n\n*Purpose:* Displays static information about the hotel (description,\ncontact details, address). No interactive signals — purely informational.\n\n*Beginner Example:* The "About Us" page on any website — you read it,\nyou don\'t interact with it.\n\n*Child Components:* None (static text and images only).\n\n---\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Reusable Components (Generic)]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 3,\n  rows: 3,\n  gutter: 0.2in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold")[Component], #text(weight: "bold")[File], #text(weight: "bold")[Role]],\n  #raw("RoomCard"), #raw("generic/RoomCard.qml"), "Room preview card with hover overlay + Book button",\n  #raw("NavLink"), #raw("generic/NavLink.qml"), "Nav link with animated underline",\n  #raw("AccentButton"), #raw("generic/AccentButton.qml"), "Gold rounded call-to-action button",\n  #raw("CustomButton"), #raw("generic/CustomButton.qml"), "Base button (text + font size)",\n  #raw("CustomRect"), #raw("generic/CustomRect.qml"), "Debug rectangle (magenta border)",\n  #raw("SearchField"), #raw("generic/SearchField.qml"), "Label + value chip (emits clicked)",\n)\n\n#pagebreak\n\n// ============================================================\n// SECTION 3 — COMPONENT TO SIGNAL FLOW\n// ============================================================\n#heading[Component to Signal Flow Mapping]\n\nThis section maps every interactive component to the signal or slot it\ntriggers. The table below is the complete inventory of "what fires what."\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Signal Map]\n\n#grid(\n  columns: 4,\n  rows: 15,\n  gutter: 0.15in,\n  [#text(weight: "bold")[Component], #text(weight: "bold")[Signal / Slot], #text(weight: "bold")[Direction], #text(weight: "bold")[Beginner Trigger Explanation]],\n  //\n  #raw("NavLink"), #raw("navigateRequested(page)"), "→ App.qml", "You click a nav link (Home, Rooms, etc.) → the app switches to that page.",\n  //\n  #raw("AccentButton"), #raw("clicked"), "→ parent page", "You click a gold button → the page that owns it hears the click and decides what to do.",\n  //\n  #raw("RoomCard"), #raw("bookRequested(roomNum, ...)"), "→ RoomListPage", "You click \'Book now\' on a room card → the room number and dates are sent up to open the reservation form.",\n  //\n  #raw("RoomCard"), #raw("clicked"), "→ parent page", "You click the card body (not the button) → the parent page could react (e.g. show details).",\n  //\n  #raw("PillButton"), #raw("clicked"), "→ parent page", "You press a pill-shaped button (Login, Register, Confirm) → the parent form reads inputs and calls a controller slot.",\n  //\n  #raw("FilterChip"), #raw("clicked"), "→ RoomListPage", "You toggle a tier chip → the page updates its internal filter state.",\n  //\n  #raw("SearchField"), #raw("clicked"), "→ parent page", "You click a search chip → the parent page could open a date picker or dropdown.",\n  //\n  #raw("FormField"), "(none — passive input)", "—", "You type into a text field. The field itself emits no signal. The parent button reads its #raw(\\"text\\") property when clicked.",\n  //\n  #raw("SummaryRow"), "(none — passive label)", "—", "Displays a calculated value. No signal.",\n  //\n  #raw("PaymentOption"), #raw("clicked"), "→ ReservationForm", "You select a payment method (Online / At Counter) → the form remembers your choice for the booking call.",\n  //\n  #raw("Login form"), #raw("controller.login(email, pass)"), "→ Python slot", "You fill in email + password and press Login → the controller checks credentials and returns an error string (empty = success).",\n  //\n  #raw("Register form"), #raw("controller.register(...)"), "→ Python slot", "You fill in name, email, phone, password and press Register → the controller creates the account.",\n  //\n  #raw("Logout button"), #raw("controller.logout()"), "→ Python slot", "You click Logout → the controller clears the current user session.",\n  //\n  #raw("Cancel button"), #raw("controller.cancelReservation(num)"), "→ Python slot", "You click Cancel on a reservation → the controller cancels it in the database.",\n)\n\n#heading(level: 2)[State Properties (Read by QML, Written by Python)]\n\nThese aren\'t signals, but they drive what the UI *shows*:\n\n| Property | Written By | Read By |\n|---|---|---|\n| #raw("controller.isLoggedIn") | Python (login/logout) | Header, ReservationListPage |\n| #raw("controller.currentUserName") | Python (login/register) | Header, ReservationForm |\n| #raw("controller.currentUserEmail") | Python (login/register) | ReservationForm |\n| #raw("controller.roomListModel") | Python (refreshRooms) | HomePage, RoomListPage, ReservationForm |\n| #raw("controller.roomTierListModel") | Python (refreshTiers) | RoomListPage |\n| #raw("controller.reservationListModel") | Python (refreshReservations) | ReservationListPage |\n\nThe only QML-emitted *signal* that Python sends back is:\n\n- #raw("controller.currentUserChanged") — emitted after login/logout/profile-change, so the QML reservation page knows to reload.\n\n#pagebreak\n\n// ============================================================\n// SECTION 4 — UI FLOW & STATE BOUNDARY DIAGRAMS\n// ============================================================\n#heading[UI Flow & State Boundary Diagrams]\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 1: Application Startup]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("Main.qml", shape: fletcher.round, text: "Main.qml"),\n  arrow("Window + Loader"),\n  node("App.qml", shape: fletcher.round, text: "App.qml", fill: luma(230)),\n  arrow("currentPage = \\"Home\\""),\n  node("HomePage\\nRoomCard grid", fill: luma(245), text: "HomePage.qml"),\n)\n\n*What happens:* #raw("Main.qml") creates the window and loads\n#raw("App.qml"). #raw("App.qml") sets the initial page to "Home", making\n#raw("HomePage.qml") visible. The home page reads\n#raw("controller.roomListModel") to populate its grid of #raw("RoomCard")\ncomponents. *No user interaction yet — pure bootstrap.*\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 2: Navigation (Page Switching)]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("Header.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "Header.qml\\nNavLink(\\"Rooms\\")"),\n  arrow("navigateRequested(\\"Rooms\\")"),\n  node("App.qml", fill: luma(230), text: "App.qml\\ncurrentPage = \\"Rooms\\""),\n  arrow("→ RoomListPage visible"),\n  node("RoomListPage.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "RoomListPage.qml"),\n)\n\n*Boundary:* The signal stops at #raw("App.qml"). No Python code is\ninvolved — pure QML-to-QML navigation. The signal payload is a string\n(the page name), and the router sets visibility accordingly.\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 3: Login (Crosses Signal Boundary)]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("LoginPage.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "LoginPage.qml\\nFormField × 2\\nPillButton"),\n  arrow("controller.login(email, password)"),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, fill: luma(220), text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", fill: red, "▸ SIGNAL BOUNDARY ◂")\n    #text(size: 8pt, fill: blue, "QML → Python")\n  ]),\n  arrow("returns error string"),\n  node("LoginPage.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "LoginPage.qml\\n(error label shows if non-empty)"),\n)\n\n*What happens:*\n1. User types email + password into #raw("FormField") components.\n2. User clicks the #raw("PillButton") ("Login").\n3. The button\'s #raw("onClicked") handler reads the text from both\n   fields and calls #raw("controller.login(email, password)").\n4. This crosses the **Signal Boundary** — a #raw("pyqtSlot") in Python\n   runs authentication logic.\n5. Python returns a string: empty means success, non-empty is an error.\n6. If non-empty, the error label on the page becomes visible with the\n   message. If empty, the controller sets #raw("currentUser") and emits\n   #raw("currentUserChanged"), and the page navigates to Home.\n\n*Beginner Explanation:* You type your username and password (like\nswiping a keycard). Pressing "Login" is like pressing the keycard\nagainst the reader. The reader (the controller slot) checks if it\'s\nvalid and either opens the door (success → navigate home) or beeps\nred (error label).\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 4: Booking a Room (Multi-Step, Multiple Boundaries)]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("RoomCard", fill: luma(245), text: "RoomCard.qml\\n\'Book now\' button"),\n  arrow("bookRequested(roomNum)"),\n  node("RoomListPage", fill: luma(245), text: "RoomListPage\\n→ navigateRequested(\\"Reserve\\")"),\n  arrow("opens ReservationForm"),\n  node("ReservationForm.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "ReservationForm.qml\\nRoomCard + FormFields + PaymentOption"),\n  arrow("controller.bookRoom(...)"),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, fill: luma(220), text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", fill: red, "▸ SIGNAL BOUNDARY ◂")\n  ]),\n  arrow("success"),\n  node("ReservationForm", fill: luma(245), text: "ReservationForm\\n→ navigateRequested(\\"Reservations\\")"),\n)\n\n*What happens (step by step):*\n1. On HomePage or RoomListPage, user hovers a #raw("RoomCard") and\n   clicks "Book now".\n2. The card emits #raw("bookRequested(roomNum, ...)"). The parent page\n   catches it, calls #raw("App.qml") to navigate to "Reserve", and\n   opens #raw("ReservationForm.qml") with the selected room pre-filled.\n3. User fills in guest details, dates, and picks a payment method.\n4. User clicks "Confirm Booking" → the form calls:\n   - #raw("controller.bookRoom(userId, roomNum, dateIn, dateOut)")\n   - #raw("controller.pay(reservationNum, method, amount)")\n   Both cross the **Signal Boundary** into Python.\n5. On success, the form navigates to the Reservations page so the user\n   can see their new booking.\n\n*Beginner Explanation:* You see a room you like in a brochure (RoomCard).\nYou tear off the slip and hand it to the front desk (bookRequested).\nThe desk gives you a form to fill (ReservationForm). You fill in your\ndetails and hand it back (controller.bookRoom). The clerk processes it\nand hands you a receipt. All the "handing off" moments are signals.\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 5: Filtering Rooms]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("RoomListPage.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "RoomListPage.qml\\nFilterChip × N\\nFilterField × 4"),\n  arrow("clicked (tier chip)"),\n  node("RoomListPage", fill: luma(245), text: "(internal state updated)"),\n  arrow("Apply → controller.setRoomFilters(...)"),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, fill: luma(220), text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", fill: red, "▸ SIGNAL BOUNDARY ◂")\n  ]),\n  arrow("Python refreshes roomListModel"),\n  node("RoomListPage", fill: luma(245), text: "RoomCard grid auto-updates\\n(via model binding)"),\n)\n\n*What happens:*\n1. User toggles tier chips and types in date/price fields. Each chip\n   click updates an internal variable; no signal crosses yet.\n2. User clicks "Apply". The page reads all filter values and calls\n   #raw("controller.setRoomFilters(...)") — crossing the boundary.\n3. Python processes the filters, updates #raw("controller.roomListModel").\n4. The #raw("RoomCard") grid, bound to the model, automatically shows\n   the new filtered set. (Qt\'s model/view binding handles this — no\n   extra signal needed.)\n\n*Beginner Explanation:* You\'re using the sliders and checkboxes on a\ncar rental website. You tweak things — nothing happens yet. You click\n"Search" (Apply), and the results update. The tweaks are local, the\nsearch crosses into the database.\n\n#heading(level: 2)[Flow 6: Reservation Cancellation]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("ReservationListPage.qml", fill: luma(245), text: "ReservationListPage.qml\\nCancel button"),\n  arrow("controller.cancelReservation(num)"),\n  node("", shape: fletcher.square, stroke: none, fill: luma(220), text: [\n    #text(size: 10pt, weight: "bold", fill: red, "▸ SIGNAL BOUNDARY ◂")\n  ]),\n  arrow("Python refreshes reservationListModel"),\n  node("ReservationListPage", fill: luma(245), text: "List auto-updates"),\n)\n\n*What happens:* User clicks Cancel → slot call → Python deletes from\nDB → model refreshes → QML list view reflects the change. Simple,\nsingle-boundary crossing.\n\n---\n\n// ============================================================\n// SUMMARY DIAGRAM — Full Page Map\n// ============================================================\n#pagebreak\n#heading[Appendix: Full Navigation Map]\n\n#fletcher.diagram(\n  node("Main.qml", shape: fletcher.round, text: "Main.qml"),\n  arrow(),\n  node("App.qml", shape: fletcher.round, fill: luma(225), text: "App.qml\\n(currentPage router)"),\n  //\n  arrow(dx: -3.5, dy: -1.5, label: "Home"),\n  node("HomePage.qml\\n+ RoomCard ×N", fill: luma(245)),\n  //\n  arrow(dx: 0, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 3, label: "Rooms"),\n  node("RoomListPage.qml\\n+ FilterChips + RoomCard ×N", fill: luma(245)),\n  arrow(dx: 3.5, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 4, label: "Reserve"),\n  node("ReservationForm.qml\\n+ FormFields + PaymentOption", fill: luma(245)),\n  arrow(dx: 0, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 5, label: "Reservations"),\n  node("ReservationListPage.qml\\n+ ListView + Cancel buttons", fill: luma(245)),\n  arrow(dx: -3.5, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 6, label: "Login"),\n  node("LoginPage.qml\\n+ FormFields + PillButton", fill: luma(245)),\n  arrow(dx: 0, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 7, label: "Register"),\n  node("RegisterPage.qml\\n+ FormFields + PillButton", fill: luma(245)),\n  arrow(dx: 3.5, dy: -1.5, from: 1, to: 8, label: "About"),\n  node("AboutPage.qml\\n(static)", fill: luma(245)),\n)\n\n#align(center, [#text(size: 9pt, style: "italic", "Every arrow is a navigateRequested() signal. Only one page is visible at a time.")])\n\n---\n\n#text(size: 9pt, fill: gray, "Generated from src/modules/gui/ — architecture document for presentation.")') with args () and kwargs {} from queue
